WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Different output contexts interpret characters differently.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.800
In an HTML body, angle brackets can start tags and ampersands can start entities.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.820
In an HTML attribute, quotes can end the attribute value.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.560
In JavaScript, quotes, backslashes, line breaks, and closing script sequences may change code structure.

5
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:28.260
In URLs, reserved characters can change paths, parameters, fragments, or schemes.

6
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:35.000
A single encoder cannot safely cover every destination because each destination has its own grammar.

7
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.160
CSS, JSON, XML, CSV, logs, and reports all have their own rules.

8
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.300
CSS values can interact with browser parsing and external references.

9
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.800
JSON should be produced through serializers, not hand-built strings.

10
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.220
XML has entity rules and structural concerns.

11
00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.040
CSV may be opened by spreadsheet software that interprets certain cell prefixes as formulas.

12
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:08.980
Logs and reports may later be rendered in web interfaces, searched, copied, or exported into other tools.

13
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.840
Output can move through multiple contexts over its lifetime.

14
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.880
The practical habit is to identify the destination before encoding.

15
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.660
Ask: where is this value going next?

16
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:30.760
Is it plain HTML text, an attribute, a URL component, JavaScript data, JSON, a style value, a report cell, or a log field?

17
00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.940
Then use an encoder or safe API designed for that destination.

18
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:43.200
Context-specific thinking prevents the common mistake of applying one familiar escaping function everywhere and assuming the result is safe.
